  "rewrittenText": "Subject: On Clear Records and Honest Work\n\nWhen work vanishes into private folders and scattered heads, teams do not move forward; they wander. Right now, our departments operate like isolated workshops, hoarding insight behind closed doors and leaving everyone else to guess in the dark. It is wasteful, exhausting, and entirely avoidable.\n\nWe do not need another layer of digital noise. We need a single, shared forge where our working knowledge lives out in the open. Fragmented notes do not preserve craft; they bury it under rubble. When an engineer departs without leaving behind a clear blueprint, someone else spends three weeks rebuilding a wall that was already standing.\n\nSet aside an hour every week. Strip the rot from your project notes and write down what actually happened. When we carve out time to document the work as we build it, we stop repeating our mistakes and begin moving with quiet, deliberate force. Build records that remember human hands were on them.",
  "changesExplanation": "The original draft suffered from classic corporate evasion, relying heavily on bureaucratic throat-clearing, passive construction, and empty MBA vocabulary ('strategic optimization', 'cross-functional teams', 'synergistic alignment', 'maximize collaborative bandwidth'). These abstractions concealed a simple, human problem: people were not writing down what they were doing, causing unnecessary friction and duplicated labor. The rewrite strips away every instance of corporate jargon and translates administrative claims into physical reality.\n\nTo mirror the Authentic Craftsman profile, sentences were restructured to balance rolling, cadenced observations with decisive staccato punches. Vague hedges like 'it is arguably imperative' and 'we should probably consider' were eliminated in favor of grounded declarative authority. Material metaphors of timber, stone, and the workshop replace sterile consulting clichés, anchoring the argument in tangible labor.\n\nThe final paragraph shifts the administrative request into a direct, rhythmic imperative. Rather than begging for buy-in to hit 'quarterly deliverables,' the call to action summons personal craftsmanship and collective responsibility, closing on a signature authorial aphorism.",
